#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ddf04c2-71d3-54b1-9f4b-d9699e9cc552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{12}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513693} {:answer/id #uuid "69f7fd71-6066-58ff-973d-5a24b0715dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513816} {:answer/id #uuid "a01e6e97-6d87-5937-8e57-076aaa390207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513942} {:answer/id #uuid "a8adc543-25c4-55be-8081-e8857819cce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513971} {:answer/id #uuid "f56d5cb2-21a5-51fe-8738-17f8affe9a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13{x}^{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f7fd71-6066-58ff-973d-5a24b0715dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513816} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8fbc5ea-5d9e-52eb-88d3-b4d6971c6c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1117fb-96ed-5032-8c50-48772b44cf5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113668" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{13}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} \\\\ &= {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0387228b-625c-57a2-b9a5-65db0be7f989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513537} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8e57bc-7acc-5bac-a92c-82592c8e49c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513823} {:answer/id #uuid "78df9ad8-f292-595e-872f-5f8ef18126cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513850} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf0e947-1093-5e50-a47b-29c991bd99cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513977} {:answer/id #uuid "b224165e-2200-5c30-be67-560f8a090e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf0e947-1093-5e50-a47b-29c991bd99cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513977} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee20556c-e250-51aa-b85c-fc28797d2901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4e1eabf-8b25-5ba2-8660-9b7cb6a5610c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29994" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{4}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} {:answer/id #uuid "0e19b2e0-2b38-57c0-b50d-3e99a4fb345c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519013} {:answer/id #uuid "12efe453-d9bc-5e34-bdeb-04e1a1d22aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe6c9c-6868-551d-8a19-e70fb67ab8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519256} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ea829-20bc-5000-ad9a-b878fad5ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" :question/math-academy-id "q-126866" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eabca83-d18a-5405-a6c6-f497cd12ba15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519740} {:answer/id #uuid "69bdaec0-113c-5f67-a3b2-6bf2fec56c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519746} {:answer/id #uuid "7a569ec5-a696-5d26-b706-23b4f0dc0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519750} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf88a3-df95-57b0-b822-5265e494325f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519752} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b1cbca-9a29-5208-8d71-1cb73e2a2f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69bdaec0-113c-5f67-a3b2-6bf2fec56c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519746} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bacee1a-6cc4-552c-a4f4-a0fab52d47ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3824a288-14f7-50ee-b550-8febd725cc36" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 12 y+\\left(24 x + 32 y\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\left(21 x + 22 y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =44 (1) + 42 (2) \\\\ & =48+\\left(80\\right) \\\\ & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8a6022-efb7-5115-88bb-57337d6e4eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519734} {:answer/id #uuid "430695f4-dc5a-53b0-9146-849d0305d94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519741} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9f0e06-8911-511e-9c02-735b11db671c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519758} {:answer/id #uuid "d43f4cc6-d224-5cf6-af6b-e7e47c04208c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519768} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8913ba-f1ef-54f0-930d-e0d7c7ed078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43f4cc6-d224-5cf6-af6b-e7e47c04208c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519768} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ed5189-349f-5ae2-9bec-beaf51162359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "479cee1b-5500-5cd2-ad8c-021a17042587" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(4 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(4 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 24 y+\\left(24 x + 18 y\\right) \\\\  & =42 \\left(x + y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =42 (1) + 42 (2) \\\\ & =60+\\left(66\\right) \\\\ & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e55554-e197-5f70-be8c-57881ebe28e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519730} {:answer/id #uuid "11a14153-868e-557f-9e7e-a963a2420b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{73}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519732} {:answer/id #uuid "675a4586-bc07-5a8e-a9c6-d9654edb99b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519745} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff0bf56-f2aa-5cad-873f-81a3ab632ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519753} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e215ca-8076-5b63-89d2-0b7ccc3a5112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{71}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01e55554-e197-5f70-be8c-57881ebe28e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519730} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d1f39b-26c1-5d7f-a544-4f6a511c0717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71332596-10b4-5473-a699-991346f64d01" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x^{2} e^{3 y}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\ln{\\left(3 x + y \\right)})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(3 x^{2} e^{3 y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3 x + y}+\\left(9 x^{2} e^{3 y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(9 x^{3} e^{3 y} + 3 x^{2} y e^{3 y} + 1\\right)}{3 x + y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,0) & =\\frac{3 \\left(3 (0) (2)^{2} e^{3 (0)} + 9 (2)^{3} e^{3 (0)} + 1\\right)}{(0) + 3 (2)} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(36\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{73}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01282e04-9f20-5ce1-b4e5-ca694fd92ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519729} {:answer/id #uuid "071fc85e-b7ec-5343-9b00-518cdab61fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519731} {:answer/id #uuid "502b73fb-c85e-530b-b012-c7169375e747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519743} {:answer/id #uuid "635b253f-5fcb-5ba4-9a33-1388e6f473f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519744} {:answer/id #uuid "7430b7b6-9ff9-5f42-804a-ff990dfb7f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "071fc85e-b7ec-5343-9b00-518cdab61fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519731} :answer-field/id #uuid "328fe164-3ff3-5e5e-b76d-41619193d616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b8c58a8-631b-5f56-81d9-dd2355b91a3c" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =18 x + 12 y+\\left(12 x + 18 y\\right) \\\\  & =30 \\left(x + y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =30 (1) + 30 (2) \\\\ & =48+\\left(42\\right) \\\\ & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be3be8e-6d9b-5c17-92a8-bd2de1598e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519733} {:answer/id #uuid "303c1efb-11a4-5345-86f6-a486861e8209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519737} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1af92f-76e8-5a22-99e6-90cd7abd1056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519762} {:answer/id #uuid "bec92d70-cf32-5e22-ae2e-a02cd8bf6559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519763} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6ee38f-b9e3-54d6-a344-8f5ee7086794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1be3be8e-6d9b-5c17-92a8-bd2de1598e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519733} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1f744b-0571-5f1c-9cbd-a75475e65e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9828b186-b4ee-5f2e-84cc-caeea0434078" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(\\left(4 x + y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\left(4 x + y\\right)^{2})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\left(3 x + 4 y\\right)^{2}) \\\\  & =32 x + 8 y+\\left(24 x + 32 y\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\left(7 x + 5 y\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(2,1) & =40 (1) + 56 (2) \\\\ & =72+\\left(80\\right) \\\\ & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71941b2e-6492-57b3-9f15-2ea08ef07538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519748} {:answer/id #uuid "98de73de-134d-534b-abc3-ac5f77866e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519755} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f39197-e7b1-5769-b28c-870aa6abc063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519756} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9a6ca6-8dbd-553d-aac4-d168e560858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519759} {:answer/id #uuid "c20e4615-a0de-539b-8d9d-86666c3a6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71941b2e-6492-57b3-9f15-2ea08ef07538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519748} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ca3c765-a8cc-5f28-93ee-3d5fd340c30b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b21cce72-05b1-50b1-986f-ae9d83039518" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot F$ at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=\\nabla\\cdot F=\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nWhen differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, hold $y$ constant; when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$, hold $x$ constant.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\cdot F & =\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(3 \\ln{\\left(x - y \\right)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x + y}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{x - y}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{x + 5 y}{\\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(3,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\cdot F)(3,1) & =- \\frac{5 (1) + (3)}{\\left(- (1) + (3)\\right) \\left((1) + (3)\\right)} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c01bee6-c22f-53b0-8e4c-2830202db2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519787} {:answer/id #uuid "4001b8b9-d69a-5393-aa8a-56dbb7b71d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}1\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519788} {:answer/id #uuid "810e6f01-13a2-54f8-a124-b8db7ad3029c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519808} {:answer/id #uuid "ec51caf8-2b6b-5744-bff0-e4556b7398b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519834} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f1c350-f5d6-57ff-b3fe-c711f8c6eee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec51caf8-2b6b-5744-bff0-e4556b7398b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519834} :answer-field/id #uuid "647d6e0d-520d-5948-bf78-b3d3e4c75a3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ba10e69-e889-58f2-81da-5e14a75e9ef5" :question/problem "![](images/26/26fffdfc736d59a285ec7beb916417657c50a48494eace96e0db5d70cd7ce522.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0a/0a8351f5cdfa59df7ec8a328c18b6e83342796728e11ff26911ce661eda1513b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b85568-6d71-5d6e-b0c0-e13be22ee1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}4 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519774} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5fa325-0187-5d8f-8b92-c7984a6bad98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519819} {:answer/id #uuid "e57a89d7-76e8-5cf7-9b59-0f813172b2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519831} {:answer/id #uuid "e70e10fe-71f2-5728-9617-73637c24f0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519832} {:answer/id #uuid "f1080001-7044-5cae-8c5e-ca6c9563b93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}-2\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e57a89d7-76e8-5cf7-9b59-0f813172b2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519831} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9f5a2a-077b-5bd1-8962-eac9dfdd211a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "442c0b16-fa87-5944-8279-ae2d47c10e31" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=3$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=1$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1-\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb32c19-5f84-5544-9090-e48b70d03e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519793} {:answer/id #uuid "580b60ef-002e-59af-a542-1b98052329fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519798} {:answer/id #uuid "b507406a-a117-557c-90c1-94673aefcc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519822} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cd7c14-dc85-513f-9915-0266e55b64cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519829} {:answer/id #uuid "f79cb302-5362-5bd3-b93a-451fdc8f9345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7cd7c14-dc85-513f-9915-0266e55b64cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519829} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb4e3ac-666f-5743-8a54-1264a234ed40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54990ea5-aa3e-570c-b3da-1d09e2497f35" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 9 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008b25e3-2816-5f9f-8cbf-dc9c78374362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519772} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7deea-09e0-5087-a672-a35e9194cf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519777} {:answer/id #uuid "298b7594-7652-50a3-b1aa-da40aaf63f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519783} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0f37e4-d000-5f37-bb34-13bd090aea98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519816} {:answer/id #uuid "fb38c3c8-9f6d-5521-abf3-3f9994882b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb38c3c8-9f6d-5521-abf3-3f9994882b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :d