4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3389ec8a-5ead-590b-9ac7-88add8627653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519784} {:answer/id #uuid "495eed6a-8a13-519f-a611-b69ba073a16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519791} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed0bc57-bfca-5e00-ba52-1de4c390c35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519794} {:answer/id #uuid "55140ddf-99ac-5982-98f8-93d907c32218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519797} {:answer/id #uuid "ba902bbe-b7e8-556a-ade5-171834fc2fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495eed6a-8a13-519f-a611-b69ba073a16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519791} :answer-field/id #uuid "061e0e7c-46a8-5dad-ab08-61f43bd85732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e01ec71-3cc7-57cc-814c-d392156f806f" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1dc30e767ab459dd4625582c1d06a14f506a8600b3b47ff0cf650e160d5f9d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/88/888f97120e097c46a7dfdcfb137fbae11b71e1606ade407f7c644468de4ede2c.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 2 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 6 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(6 x^{2} + 2 x y - 6 y^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 6 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2511112b-841c-51d6-8d4d-b3539a193a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519782} {:answer/id #uuid "527a7dca-4a36-570b-a68e-a02e1073c9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519795} {:answer/id #uuid "669ada49-68c4-5dcc-b8ee-035124baf168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519805} {:answer/id #uuid "7b47de44-c51e-5c10-8ad0-40e138ed665b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}6 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519806} {:answer/id #uuid "c8807c08-a1d5-52a7-854d-06af06353c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}-2\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669ada49-68c4-5dcc-b8ee-035124baf168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519805} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4892de7-98ec-52b0-b09f-85ff129235db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcd4852-bd78-56bb-b0b5-886d7bd31914" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=4$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(4 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(4\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1120e481-e454-5ed8-9848-d43606c711d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}- 3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519778} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba9dbe8-0bd6-5c6f-805b-f866cec39b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519813} {:answer/id #uuid "95e5225f-bd3e-5c05-b72d-3618f5471707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519815} {:answer/id #uuid "c21e252e-e313-5930-bdac-6fd13a6cad09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}5 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519824} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb546e9-faf7-52eb-b1ea-ecf0a1ef22cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95e5225f-bd3e-5c05-b72d-3618f5471707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519815} :answer-field/id #uuid "62acbc22-b618-5647-b810-b46d46c380b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e830914b-72a8-5550-9a2a-83a9bba0628e" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c7fc0c4d92c68dad3d8607f25f7be80d79ca7b2d412b67b0942a669725e3d36.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=4$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4b3e6446a0ae02a08e91d527f402287151ddb3f39c7b744503430cb9697e03a.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(4-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 5,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(3\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd0f410-6e9b-5a13-84b6-2793313eb013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520005} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520009} {:answer/id #uuid "75d7df1e-163d-51cf-9003-0d07b95639d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520161} {:answer/id #uuid "86d89cd3-8e55-5f29-83ed-c409f606d553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520189} {:answer/id #uuid "996b8fd1-b20a-565b-98d1-c22e6ee7d68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520009} :answer-field/id #uuid "686cff4f-8265-5ba3-8560-bcdf5671d8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-71952" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf96aed-c4c2-50a0-9312-269557ced95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520029} {:answer/id #uuid "4793072d-d61e-5f93-bb0c-993627ee72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520100} {:answer/id #uuid "99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520209} {:answer/id #uuid "d2298536-2087-59b8-a435-384b956031dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520286} {:answer/id #uuid "e7514498-fddf-54d8-9478-ff10ceb72766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520209} :answer-field/id #uuid "44da7608-9cb4-57b2-9694-53ee352f7833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" :question/math-academy-id "q-107296" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{3}-9x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{3}-9x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520153} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b7fd03c-605a-5e8e-8867-8d3e258ab8cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-314207" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520108} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520218} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520281} {:answer/id #uuid "edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :question/math-academy-id "q-107303" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068} {:answer/id #uuid "961a1fb5-7dbb-4d31-93ea-cbe5abcbdc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-314192" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}\\right)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520739} {:answer/id #uuid "631f9a35-a275-5f5b-a43f-98f0ce6e551d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520741} {:answer/id #uuid "6c914306-27c3-556d-835c-7f1812c10b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520742} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7a9e73-8239-57a5-91ac-ff32d7a2cede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520743} {:answer/id #uuid "e74084bf-bce3-5cce-92df-5af7ef7afc55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aeda563-1ee7-5754-b7de-2251839a6fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520739} :answer-field/id #uuid "13733091-104b-5bc3-8d88-b58877c178b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25491ab2-3faf-524d-80c3-eb7b98861b8b" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y},\\,4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=8 x y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=8 x y.\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}s &=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(16 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x^{2} y\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\left[12 y^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb0108c7-f260-5b88-8929-4ad20ba02b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb0108c7-f260-5b88-8929-4ad20ba02b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522918} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1bc6060-136b-55f3-b65d-3d586b32c846" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1aeeb45a-51fe-5f7f-b6b3-81fb614e69f4" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{3t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-3t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{3t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{3t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-3t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{3t}+ 3t(3e^{3t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)(-3e^{-3t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+3t)e^{3t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}}{3(1+3t)e^{3t}}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96bfdd12-5356-5e4e-ad0c-9ecbcd9ab433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1t^2+8t)(t+1)^2}{1(t+4)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96bfdd12-5356-5e4e-ad0c-9ecbcd9ab433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1t^2+8t)(t+1)^2}{1(t+4)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522911} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfb02a6-9b02-5883-876f-ce117dbd3f4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85e9a979-dd16-5177-b9bf-cda7e7a25733" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1t}{t+1},\\quad y=\\frac{1t^2}{t+4},\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)(t+1)-1t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+1\\right)\\right)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2\\right)\\right)(t+4)-1t^2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+4\\right)\\right)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1(t+1)-1t(1)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t(t+4)-1t^2(1)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1t+1-1t}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t^2+8t-1t^2}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}}{\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}}=\\frac{(1t^2+8t)(t+1)^2}{1(t+4)^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499737b3-a0fe-585c-b187-55fbf62e6658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{3(1+1t)}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499737b3-a0fe-585c-b187-55fbf62e6658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{3(1+1t)}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522903} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1552876-53e6-5070-82ee-064a196e5b3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d686c858-3d09-57c7-a18a-662015e75d19" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{1t}+ 3t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{3(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{3(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83613d66-5728-523f-8796-7b433d105da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{2(1+1t)}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83613d66-5728-523f-8796-7b433d105da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{2(1+1t)}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522907} :answer-field/id #uuid "94172dc7-c295-558c-9fb1-935effecdd7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8e50997-ca79-5fb3-921b-0f38c140cd4d" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=2t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+2t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2e^{1t}+ 2t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{2(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{2(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed4663-b0ee-5724-a928-4ddbdc93495c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3 e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522900} {:answer/id #uuid "3e926e96-79ee-54b0-b8f8-04808f9e654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522902} {:answer/id #uuid "794828fb-0efa-5c48-a874-43e81f7e2e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522906} {:answer/id #uuid "905639c9-30eb-5d8e-80f3-36df16605295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522909} {:answer/id #uuid "e69a3702-8f8e-5204-96a2-4bfcb62090f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3 e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ed4663-b0ee-5724-a928-4ddbdc93495c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3 e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a328b167-a55b-5a07-9293-d01431385c90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f38d491a-6dbf-5ea6-9e1d-28ccc7170e0f" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0a61578b1ef5383e5e8e9e23c3c6309ede794d6b4f9c47a7ce8103b02f79fe2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=6\\left(2t{e}^{t}\\right),\\,y=4\\left({t}^{2}+2t\\right),\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\left(2t+2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\left(2t+2\\right)}{6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right)}=\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{t}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left(e\\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
